  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$",
